<title>SURINAM: Labor deal reached at Alcoa Suriname unit.</title>
<headline>Labor deal reached at Alcoa Suriname unit.</headline>
<dateline>PARAMARIBO, Suriname 1996-12-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Management and union leaders at Alcoa's Suriname unit Suralco have reached a labor contract deal which will see wages increase 150 percent over the next two years, union officials said.</p>
<p>The agreement ended a year of tense negotiations which frequently broke down in strikes.</p>
<p>Suralco has said industrial actions have cost the company about $11 million in lost output this year.</p>
<p>Suralco, 60 percent owned by Alcoa and 40 percent by Australia's Western Mining, produces an average 4,500 tonnes of alumina per day.</p>
<p>About 95 percent of its production is exported, mostly to the United States. The company's exports account for about 75 percent, or $300 million, of Suriname's total export earnings.</p>
<p>-- Caracas newsroom, 582 505 26000</p>
</text>
